<h2>A five-year-old FIR</h2>
<p>The FIR was registered on 30 January 2021 under Sections 376, 406 and 506 IPC — rape, criminal breach of trust and criminal intimidation. By the time the quashing petition was heard in November 2026, the complainant had rebuilt her life: she married another person on 8 December 2024, and she told the Court that she had originally contacted the petitioner through her own account on a matrimonial website. The parties had executed a settlement agreement on 2 September 2026 for Rs 15,50,000, of which Rs 10,00,000 stood paid; the balance of Rs 5,50,000 was paid by a demand draft dated 17 November 2026, which the complainant accepted before the Court.</p>

<h2>The legal framework the Court applied</h2>
<p>Justice Mini Pushkarna anchored the decision in the settled trilogy on inherent-power quashing. <em>Gian Singh v. State of Punjab</em>, (2012) 10 SCC 303, and <em>Parbatbhai Aahir v. State of Gujarat</em>, (2017) 9 SCC 641, establish that the High Court may quash even non-compoundable offences on settlement where the dispute is essentially private and continuing the prosecution would be oppressive or futile — while heinous offences ordinarily stand outside that power. For offences under Section 376 IPC specifically, the Court cited the Supreme Court&rsquo;s recent statement in <em>Madhukar v. State of Maharashtra</em>, 2025 SCC OnLine SC 1415: quashing in such cases is discouraged, but not barred.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The complainant&rsquo;s own stand.</strong> She appeared, confirmed the settlement and the payments, and stated she did not wish to pursue the case — the single most important factor in settlement-based quashing.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Verification.</strong> The investigating officer was present and identified both parties, foreclosing any doubt about who had settled with whom.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Futility of trial.</strong> With the complainant unwilling, a 2021 case going to trial promised years more of proceedings with little realistic prospect of conviction.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The State&rsquo;s position.</strong> The Additional Public Prosecutor raised no serious objection on the facts presented.</div>
</div>

<h2>Costs for the system&rsquo;s time</h2>
<p>The Court noted that the FIR had been pending since 2021 and that the machinery of the State had been engaged for a prolonged period. Quashing was therefore made conditional on the petitioner paying Rs 20,000 to the Bar Council of Delhi Indigent and Disabled Lawyers Fund within two weeks. Conditional-costs orders of this kind are now a regular feature of settlement quashing in Delhi: the private dispute may be resolved, but the public resources consumed are acknowledged in the order itself.</p>

<h2>What practitioners should take from it</h2>
<p>Settlement-based quashing in serious offences is never a formality. The petition succeeds on the quality of its record: a written settlement agreement, proof that every rupee promised has actually moved, the complainant&rsquo;s personal presence and unambiguous statement, and the investigating officer&rsquo;s identification of the parties. Where any of these is missing — payment partly outstanding, the complainant absent or equivocal — courts routinely adjourn or decline. Equally, the decision should not be over-read: the Court applied a discretion that remains exceptional in offences of this gravity, and nothing in it suggests that settlements can purchase closure as of right.</p>
<div class="note">Sections 376, 406 and 506 IPC correspond, for offences committed after 1 July 2024, to Sections 64, 316 and 351 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 respectively. The power to quash is now in Section 528 BNSS. Every quashing petition turns on its own record and the court&rsquo;s discretion; no outcome can be promised in any case.</div>
